Window</h1></header><div class=3D"style-module-scss-module__ubvNPq__problem=
Description"><p>There's a sliding window of size <code>k</code> that slides=
 through an integer array from left to right. Create a new array that recor=
ds the <strong>largest number found in each window</strong> as it slides th=
rough.</p><h4 id=3D"example">Example:</h4><figure class=3D"py-1"><div style=
=3D"display:flex;justify-content:center"><img alt=3D"Image represents a ste=
p-by-step illustration of finding the maximum value within an array of inte=
gers.  Four rows depict the array `[3, 2, 4, 1, 2, 1, 1]`. In each row, a l=
ight-blue rectangular highlight progressively moves across the array, indic=
ating the current element being considered as the potential maximum.  To th=
e right of each array, 'max =3D ' is followed by the maximum value found so=
 far during that step. The first three rows show the highlight moving from =
the leftmost element (3) to the element 4, correctly identifying 4 as the m=
aximum. The final row shows the highlight moving to the last element (1) af=
ter 4 has already been identified as the maximum, resulting in a final maxi=
mum value of 4.  The image visually demonstrates a linear scan algorithm fo=
r finding the maximum element in an array." width=3D"352" height=3D"147" de=
coding=3D"async" data-nimg=3D"1" style=3D"color:transparent" src=3D"https:/=
/bytebytego.com/images/courses/coding-patterns/stacks/maximums-of-sliding-w=
indow/maximums-of-sliding-window-ZTLN5HJK.svg?dpl=3Ddpl_CEjisbLnWxFQGUTRjiH=
GoKY1oV5c"></div></figure><pre><code class=3D"hljs language-python">Input: =
nums =3D [<span class=3D"hljs-number">3</span>, <span class=3D"hljs-number"=
>2</span>, <span class=3D"hljs-number">4</span>, <span class=3D"hljs-number=
">1</span>, <span class=3D"hljs-number">2</span>, <span class=3D"hljs-numbe=
r">1</span>, <span class=3D"hljs-number">1</span>], k =3D <span class=3D"hl=
js-number">4</span>
Output: [<span class=3D"hljs-number">4</span>, <span class=3D"hljs-number">=
4</span>, <span class=3D"hljs-number">4</span>, <span class=3D"hljs-number"=
>2</span>]
</code></pre></div>
<h2 id=3D"intuition">Intuition</h2>
<p>A brute-force approach to solving this problem involves iterating throug=
h each element within a window to find the maximum value of that window. Re=
peating this for each window will take <span class=3D"katex"><span class=3D=
"katex-mathml"><math xmlns=3D"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantic=
s><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=3D"false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>=E2=8B=85</mo>=
<mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=3D"false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=3D"appli=
cation/x-tex">O(n\cdot k)</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=
=3D"katex-html" aria-hidden=3D"true"><span class=3D"base"><span class=3D"st=
rut" style=3D"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em"></span><span class=3D"mord=
 mathnormal" style=3D"margin-right:0.02778em">O</span><span class=3D"mopen"=
>(</span><span class=3D"mord mathnormal">n</span><span class=3D"mspace" sty=
le=3D"margin-right:0.2222em"></span><span class=3D"mbin">=E2=8B=85</span><s=
pan class=3D"mspace" style=3D"margin-right:0.2222em"></span></span><span cl=
ass=3D"base"><span class=3D"strut" style=3D"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25=
em"></span><span class=3D"mord mathnormal" style=3D"margin-right:0.03148em"=
>k</span><span class=3D"mclose">)</span></span></span></span> time because =
we traverse <span class=3D"katex"><span class=3D"katex-mathml"><math xmlns=
=3D"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow><=
annotation encoding=3D"application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>=
</span><span class=3D"katex-html" aria-hidden=3D"true"><span class=3D"base"=
><span class=3D"strut" style=3D"height:0.6944em"></span><span class=3D"mord=
 mathnormal" style=3D"margin-right:0.03148em">k</span></span></span></span>=
 elements for up to <span class=3D"katex"><span class=3D"katex-mathml"><mat=
h xmlns=3D"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><=
/mrow><annotation encoding=3D"application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics>=
</math></span><span class=3D"katex-html" aria-hidden=3D"true"><span class=
=3D"base"><span class=3D"strut" style=3D"height:0.4306em"></span><span clas=
s=3D"mord mathnormal">n</span></span></span></span> windows.</p>
<p>The main issue is that as we slide the window, we keep re-examining the =
same elements we've already looked at in previous windows. This is because =
two adjacent windows share mostly the same values.</p>
<figure class=3D"py-1"><div style=3D"display:flex;justify-content:center"><=
img alt=3D"Image represents two nearly identical arrays of integers, each e=
nclosed in square brackets.  The top array is `[3, 2, 4, 1, 2, 1, 1]` and t=
he bottom array is `[3, 2, 4, 1, 2, 1, 1]`. A light-blue rectangular box hi=
ghlights the sub-array `[2, 4, 1]` in both arrays, indicating that these el=
ements have the same values in both arrays. The text 'same values' is posit=
ioned within the central area of the box, explicitly labeling the highlight=
ed section.  The arrays are horizontally aligned, with the highlighted sect=
ions overlapping vertically.  No URLs or parameters are present." loading=
=3D"lazy" width=3D"265" height=3D"105" decoding=3D"async" data-nimg=3D"1" s=
tyle=3D"color:transparent" src=3D"https://bytebytego.com/images/courses/cod=
ing-patterns/stacks/maximums-of-sliding-window/image-07-06-1-ZWBRMHOR.svg?d=
pl=3Ddpl_CEjisbLnWxFQGUTRjiHGoKY1oV5c"></div></figure>
<p>A more efficient solution likely involves keeping track of values we see=
 in any given window so that at the next window, we don't have to iterate o=
ver previously seen values again. Specifically, at each window, we should o=
nly maintain a record of values that have the potential to become the maxim=
um of a future window. Let's call these values <strong>candidates</strong>,=
 where all the values that aren't candidates can no longer contribute to a =
maximum. How can we determine which numbers are candidates?</p>
<p>Consider the window of size 4 in the following array. We'll use left and=
 right pointers to define the window:</p>
